# 32. Real-World Example — Private Password File

Imagine a server has:

```text
database-password.txt
```

Bad permissions:

```text
-rw-r--r-- database-password.txt
```

Other users may be able to read it.

A more restrictive permission might be:

```bash
chmod 600 database-password.txt
```

Result:

```text
-rw-------
```

Now the file is restricted to the owner through the normal Unix permission bits.

---

# 33. Real-World Example — Web Application

Imagine a website contains:

```text
/var/www/html/
```

A web application may need:

```text
read access
```

to certain files but should not necessarily have unrestricted write access.

Giving every file:

```text
777
```

would create unnecessary risk.

The principle is:

> Give users and processes only the permissions they actually need.

This is called the **Principle of Least Privilege**.

---

# 34. Principle of Least Privilege

The principle of least privilege means:

> A user, program, or service should receive only the minimum permissions required to perform its job.

Example:

```text
Web server
   ↓
Needs to read website files
   ↓
Give required read/traverse access
```

Instead of:

```text
Web server
   ↓
Full read/write/execute access everywhere
```

Least privilege reduces the potential impact of a compromise.

